      <!-- SECTION 03 EN -->
      <h2 id="how-salesforce-processes">03 · HOW DOES SALESFORCE PROCESS AN EMAIL WITH AMPSCRIPT?</h2>
      <p>To understand AMPscript without diving into the deep internal architecture of Salesforce, we can represent the process using the following simplified conceptual flow:</p>

      <div style="background:var(--bg-card); border:1px solid var(--border-dim); border-radius:var(--radius); padding:1.5rem; margin:1.5rem 0;">
        <div style="font-family:var(--font-arcade); font-size:0.5rem; color:var(--neon-pink); margin-bottom:1rem;">🔄 CONCEPTUAL PROCESSING PIPELINE</div>
        
        <div style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:0.9rem; font-size:0.88rem;">
          <div style="display:flex; align-items:flex-start; gap:0.8rem;">
            <span style="background:var(--neon-cyan); color:#000; font-family:var(--font-arcade); font-size:0.5rem; padding:0.2rem 0.5rem; border-radius:3px; flex-shrink:0;">STEP 1</span>
            <div><strong>Salesforce loads subscriber context:</strong> The current subscriber record is loaded from the source Data Extension or send list.</div>
          </div>

          <div style="display:flex; align-items:flex-start; gap:0.8rem;">
            <span style="background:var(--neon-pink); color:#fff; font-family:var(--font-arcade); font-size:0.5rem; padding:0.2rem 0.5rem; border-radius:3px; flex-shrink:0;">STEP 2</span>
            <div><strong>AMPscript accesses available data:</strong> The engine parses the code, initializes variables, and retrieves attributes or related records.</div>
          </div>

          <div style="display:flex; align-items:flex-start; gap:0.8rem;">
            <span style="background:var(--neon-yellow); color:#000; font-family:var(--font-arcade); font-size:0.5rem; padding:0.2rem 0.5rem; border-radius:3px; flex-shrink:0;">STEP 3</span>
            <div><strong>Functions and conditions are evaluated:</strong> Logical structures (<code>IF/ELSE</code>) and formatting or validation functions execute.</div>
          </div>

          <div style="display:flex; align-items:flex-start; gap:0.8rem;">
            <span style="background:var(--neon-purple); color:#fff; font-family:var(--font-arcade); font-size:0.5rem; padding:0.2rem 0.5rem; border-radius:3px; flex-shrink:0;">STEP 4</span>
            <div><strong>Personalized content is generated:</strong> Inline output tags like <code>%%=v(@name)=%%</code> are substituted with the rendered text or HTML.</div>
          </div>

          <div style="display:flex; align-items:flex-start; gap:0.8rem;">
            <span style="background:var(--neon-green); color:#000; font-family:var(--font-arcade); font-size:0.5rem; padding:0.2rem 0.5rem; border-radius:3px; flex-shrink:0;">STEP 5</span>
            <div><strong>Salesforce produces final message:</strong> The finalized personalized message is produced for delivery to the recipient.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="callout tip">
        <div class="callout-label">💡 PRO TIP · EMAIL COMPONENT PROCESSING ORDER</div>
        Marketing Cloud processes email components in this order: <strong>Preheader → HTML Body → Text Body → Subject Line</strong>. Because of this, a variable defined earlier in the process can be available in components processed afterward (for example, setting a variable in the HTML body to reuse it in the subject line).
      </div>

      <div class="callout">
        <div class="callout-label">📌 PRECISION · CASE SENSITIVITY</div>
        Keywords and function names in AMPscript <strong>are case-insensitive</strong> (for example, <code>SET</code>, <code>Set</code>, and <code>set</code> are accepted by the engine). Nevertheless, adhering to a consistent convention is recommended to keep code readable and maintainable.
      </div>

      <div class="callout tip">
        <div class="callout-label">💡 IN A NUTSHELL</div>
        Salesforce executes AMPscript server-side to assemble and tailor each individual message before delivery.
      </div>

      <!-- SECTION 04 EN -->
      <h2 id="basic-syntax">04 · BASIC SYNTAX AND DELIMITERS</h2>
      <p>The two primary ways you will write AMPscript most frequently are:</p>

      <h3>1. Code Blocks: <code>%%[ ... ]%%</code></h3>
      <p><code>%%[ ... ]%%</code> code blocks are primarily used to execute logic, declare variables, prepare data, perform calculations, and evaluate conditions.</p>
      <p>Statements such as <code>VAR</code>, <code>SET</code>, and <code>IF</code> do not generate visible content on their own. Typically, inline AMPscript (<code>%%= =%%</code>) is used to output results, although AMPscript also provides functions such as <code>Output()</code>, which allow inserting the result of another function into the rendered content from within a code block.</p>

      <div class="code-block">
        <div class="code-block-header">
          <div class="code-block-dots"><div class="code-block-dot"></div><div class="code-block-dot"></div><div class="code-block-dot"></div></div>
          <span class="code-block-lang">AMPscript · Code Block</span>
          <button class="code-copy-btn">📋 Copy</button>
        </div>
        <pre><span class="cm">%%[
  /* 1. Variable declaration */</span>
  <span class="kw">VAR</span> <span class="vr">@name</span>, <span class="vr">@tier</span>, <span class="vr">@discount</span>

  <span class="cm">/* 2. Value assignment */</span>
  <span class="kw">SET</span> <span class="vr">@name</span> = <span class="fn">AttributeValue</span>(<span class="st">"FirstName"</span>)
  <span class="kw">SET</span> <span class="vr">@tier</span> = <span class="fn">AttributeValue</span>(<span class="st">"Tier"</span>)

  <span class="cm">/* 3. Conditional logic (comparison using ==) */</span>
  <span class="kw">IF</span> <span class="vr">@tier</span> == <span class="st">"VIP"</span> <span class="kw">THEN</span>
    <span class="kw">SET</span> <span class="vr">@discount</span> = <span class="nb">20</span>
  <span class="kw">ELSE</span>
    <span class="kw">SET</span> <span class="vr">@discount</span> = <span class="nb">5</span>
  <span class="kw">ENDIF</span>
<span class="cm">]%%</span></pre>
      </div>

      <h3>2. Inline Output: <code>%%= ... =%%</code></h3>
      <p>Used directly inside HTML markup or plain text to <strong>print the value of a variable or function result</strong> into that exact position:</p>

      <div class="code-block">
        <div class="code-block-header">
          <div class="code-block-dots"><div class="code-block-dot"></div><div class="code-block-dot"></div><div class="code-block-dot"></div></div>
          <span class="code-block-lang">HTML + Inline AMPscript</span>
          <button class="code-copy-btn">📋 Copy</button>
        </div>
        <pre><span class="cm">&lt;!-- Inline output in HTML body --&gt;</span>
<span class="kw">&lt;h1&gt;</span>Hello, <span class="st">%%=v(@name)=%%</span>!<span class="kw">&lt;/h1&gt;</span>
<span class="kw">&lt;p&gt;</span>Your current status is <span class="kw">&lt;strong&gt;</span><span class="st">%%=v(@tier)=%%</span><span class="kw">&lt;/strong&gt;</span>.<span class="kw">&lt;/p&gt;</span>
<span class="kw">&lt;p&gt;</span>You have a <span class="kw">&lt;span style="color:#00ffff;"&gt;</span><span class="st">%%=v(@discount)=%%</span>% discount<span class="kw">&lt;/span&gt;</span> available.<span class="kw">&lt;/p&gt;</span></pre>
      </div>

      <div class="callout">
        <div class="callout-label">📌 ADDITIONAL SYNTAX NOTE</div>
        Salesforce also supports script-tag based syntax like <code>&lt;script runat="server" language="ampscript"&gt;...&lt;/script&gt;</code>, but standard blocks <code>%%[ ]%%</code> and inline tags <code>%%= =%%</code> are the everyday convention in Email Studio and Content Builder.
      </div>

      <div class="callout tip">
        <div class="callout-label">💡 IN A NUTSHELL</div>
        Use <code>%%[ ... ]%%</code> to process server-side logic and <code>%%= ... =%%</code> to render dynamic values into your message.
      </div>

      <!-- SECTION 05 EN -->
      <h2 id="variables-operators">05 · VARIABLES, ASSIGNMENT (=) AND COMPARISON (==)</h2>
      <p>Understanding how variables are created, assigned, and compared is the foundation of AMPscript development.</p>

      <h3>1. Declaration with <code>VAR</code> and Assignment with <code>SET</code></h3>
      <p>In AMPscript, every variable name begins with the <code>@</code> character:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><code>VAR</code> declares the variable (announces to the system that the variable will exist).</li>
        <li><code>SET</code> assigns a value to the variable using the <code>=</code> operator.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="callout">
        <div class="callout-label">📦 THE BOX ANALOGY</div>
        <strong>VAR</strong> creates an empty box and places a label on it (e.g., <code>@firstName</code>).<br>
        <strong>SET</strong> takes a value (e.g., <code>"Anna"</code>) and places it inside that labeled box using the <code>=</code> operator.
      </div>

      <p>Explicitly declaring variables with <code>VAR</code> makes code clearer, helps identify what variables a script utilizes, and significantly improves long-term maintainability.</p>

      <h3>2. The Critical Difference: Assignment (<code>=</code>) vs Comparison (<code>==</code>)</h3>
      <p>This is one of the most vital rules for every beginner to master:</p>

      <div style="display:grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(260px, 1fr)); gap: 1rem; margin: 1.5rem 0;">
        <div style="background:var(--bg-elevated); border:1px solid var(--border-dim); border-left:3px solid var(--neon-cyan); border-radius:var(--radius); padding:1.2rem;">
          <div style="font-family:var(--font-arcade); font-size:0.55rem; color:var(--neon-cyan); margin-bottom:0.4rem;">= · ASSIGNMENT</div>
          <p style="font-size:0.85rem; color:var(--text-secondary); margin:0 0 0.5rem 0;">Used with <code>SET</code> to <strong>store</strong> a value into a variable.</p>
          <code style="color:var(--neon-cyan); font-size:0.85rem;">SET @tier = "VIP"</code>
          <p style="font-size:0.8rem; color:var(--text-tertiary); margin:0.4rem 0 0 0;"><em>Meaning: "Store VIP inside @tier."</em></p>
        </div>

        <div style="background:var(--bg-elevated); border:1px solid var(--border-dim); border-left:3px solid var(--neon-pink); border-radius:var(--radius); padding:1.2rem;">
          <div style="font-family:var(--font-arcade); font-size:0.55rem; color:var(--neon-pink); margin-bottom:0.4rem;">== · EQUALITY COMPARISON</div>
          <p style="font-size:0.85rem; color:var(--text-secondary); margin:0 0 0.5rem 0;">Used inside <code>IF</code> to <strong>check if two values match</strong>.</p>
          <code style="color:var(--neon-pink); font-size:0.85rem;">IF @tier == "VIP" THEN</code>
          <p style="font-size:0.8rem; color:var(--text-tertiary); margin:0.4rem 0 0 0;"><em>Meaning: "Check if @tier equals VIP."</em></p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="callout tip">
        <div class="callout-label">💡 ANALOGY</div>
        <code>=</code> is like placing an item inside a box.<br>
        <code>==</code> is like checking whether what is inside a box matches a specific value.
      </div>

      <h3>3. AMPscript Operators Reference</h3>
      <p>Below are the exact operators you will write in your scripts:</p>

      <div style="background:var(--bg-elevated); border:1px solid var(--border-dim); border-radius:var(--radius); padding:1.2rem; margin:1.5rem 0;">
        <div style="font-family:var(--font-arcade); font-size:0.5rem; color:var(--neon-cyan); margin-bottom:0.8rem;">⚙️ COMPARISON & LOGICAL OPERATORS</div>
        <div style="overflow-x:auto;">
          <table style="width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; font-size:0.85rem;">
            <thead>
              <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border-dim); color:var(--text-primary); text-align:left;">
                <th style="padding:0.5rem 0.8rem;">Operator</th>
                <th style="padding:0.5rem 0.8rem;">Meaning</th>
                <th style="padding:0.5rem 0.8rem;">Code Example</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody style="color:var(--text-secondary);">
              <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.05);">
                <td style="padding:0.5rem 0.8rem; font-family:var(--font-mono); color:var(--neon-green);">==</td>
                <td style="padding:0.5rem 0.8rem;">Equal to</td>
                <td style="padding:0.5rem 0.8rem;"><code>IF @tier == "VIP" THEN</code></td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.05);">
                <td style="padding:0.5rem 0.8rem; font-family:var(--font-mono); color:var(--neon-green);">!=</td>
                <td style="padding:0.5rem 0.8rem;">Not equal to</td>
                <td style="padding:0.5rem 0.8rem;"><code>IF @country != "US" THEN</code></td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.05);">
                <td style="padding:0.5rem 0.8rem; font-family:var(--font-mono); color:var(--neon-green);">&gt;</td>
                <td style="padding:0.5rem 0.8rem;">Greater than</td>
                <td style="padding:0.5rem 0.8rem;"><code>IF @points &gt; 100 THEN</code></td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.05);">
                <td style="padding:0.5rem 0.8rem; font-family:var(--font-mono); color:var(--neon-green);">&lt;</td>
                <td style="padding:0.5rem 0.8rem;">Less than</td>
                <td style="padding:0.5rem 0.8rem;"><code>IF @balance &lt; 0 THEN</code></td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.05);">
                <td style="padding:0.5rem 0.8rem; font-family:var(--font-mono); color:var(--neon-green);">&gt;=</td>
                <td style="padding:0.5rem 0.8rem;">Greater than or equal to</td>
                <td style="padding:0.5rem 0.8rem;"><code>IF @age &gt;= 18 THEN</code></td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.05);">
                <td style="padding:0.5rem 0.8rem; font-family:var(--font-mono); color:var(--neon-green);">&lt;=</td>
                <td style="padding:0.5rem 0.8rem;">Less than or equal to</td>
                <td style="padding:0.5rem 0.8rem;"><code>IF @attempts &lt;= 3 THEN</code></td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.05);">
                <td style="padding:0.5rem 0.8rem; font-family:var(--font-mono); color:var(--neon-pink);">AND</td>
                <td style="padding:0.5rem 0.8rem;">Logical AND (both must be true)</td>
                <td style="padding:0.5rem 0.8rem;"><code>IF @points &gt; 50 AND @isActive == "True" THEN</code></td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.05);">
                <td style="padding:0.5rem 0.8rem; font-family:var(--font-mono); color:var(--neon-pink);">OR</td>
                <td style="padding:0.5rem 0.8rem;">Logical OR (at least one true)</td>
                <td style="padding:0.5rem 0.8rem;"><code>IF @tier == "VIP" OR @points &gt; 500 THEN</code></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="padding:0.5rem 0.8rem; font-family:var(--font-mono); color:var(--neon-pink);">NOT</td>
                <td style="padding:0.5rem 0.8rem;">Logical NOT (negates condition)</td>
                <td style="padding:0.5rem 0.8rem;"><code>IF NOT Empty(@name) THEN</code></td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="callout tip">
        <div class="callout-label">💡 MARKETING CLOUD ARCADE EDUCATIONAL CONVENTION</div>
        To maintain structured and readable scripts, in Marketing Cloud Arcade we frequently adopt prefixes such as <code>@s_</code> for strings, <code>@n_</code> for numbers, and <code>@b_</code> for booleans. Please note this is an educational convention to assist your learning journey, not an official requirement enforced by Salesforce.
      </div>

      <div class="callout tip">
        <div class="callout-label">💡 IN A NUTSHELL</div>
        <code>=</code> assigns a value to a variable; <code>==</code> compares two values within an equality condition.
      </div>

      <!-- SECTION 06 EN -->
      <h2 id="control-flow">06 · CONTROL FLOW: IF / ELSE CONDITIONAL STATEMENTS</h2>
      <p>Conditional statements allow you to evaluate business rules and adapt message content based on each subscriber's profile:</p>

      <div class="callout">
        <div class="callout-label">🚪 THE GATEKEEPER ANALOGY</div>
        An <code>IF</code> statement acts like a gatekeeper at an entrance: it evaluates whether a visitor meets an access rule (condition) and decides which path or perk they should receive.
      </div>

      <div class="code-block">
        <div class="code-block-header">
          <div class="code-block-dots"><div class="code-block-dot"></div><div class="code-block-dot"></div><div class="code-block-dot"></div></div>
          <span class="code-block-lang">AMPscript · IF / ELSEIF / ELSE</span>
          <button class="code-copy-btn">📋 Copy</button>
        </div>
        <pre><span class="cm">%%[
  VAR @segment, @message

  SET @segment = AttributeValue("CustomerSegment")

  IF @segment == "Platinum" THEN
    SET @message = "Exclusive VIP lounge access and unlimited free shipping."
  ELSEIF @segment == "Gold" THEN
    SET @message = "Enjoy a 15% discount on your next purchase."
  ELSE
    SET @message = "Earn reward points on every order to level up."
  ENDIF
]%%</span>

<span class="cm">&lt;!-- In email template --&gt;</span>
<span class="kw">&lt;div class="promo-box"&gt;</span>
  <span class="kw">&lt;p&gt;</span><span class="st">%%=v(@message)=%%</span><span class="kw">&lt;/p&gt;</span>
<span class="kw">&lt;/div&gt;</span></pre>
      </div>

      <div class="callout tip">
        <div class="callout-label">💡 IN A NUTSHELL</div>
        Conditionals evaluate business logic to deliver tailored content to every recipient.
      </div>

      <!-- SECTION 07 EN -->
      <h2 id="essential-functions">07 · ESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS FOR YOUR EVERYDAY WORKFLOW</h2>
      <p>Key fundamental functions every Marketing Cloud developer must know for defensive personalization:</p>

      <div style="display:grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr)); gap: 1.25rem; margin: 1.5rem 0;">
        
        <!-- FUNCTION 1: AttributeValue -->
        <div style="background:var(--bg-card); border:1px solid var(--border-dim); border-radius:var(--radius); padding:1.25rem; border-top:3px solid var(--neon-cyan);">
          <div style="font-family:var(--font-mono); font-size:0.95rem; color:var(--neon-cyan); font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.4rem;">AttributeValue("Field")</div>
          <p style="font-size:0.85rem; color:var(--text-secondary); margin:0 0 0.5rem 0;"><strong>What it does:</strong> Retrieves the attribute value from the available subscriber context. If it finds no data for that attribute, it returns <code>null</code>.</p>
          <div style="background:rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding:0.5rem; border-radius:4px; font-family:var(--font-mono); font-size:0.75rem; color:var(--text-primary); margin-bottom:0.5rem;">
            SET @name = AttributeValue("FirstName")<br>
            IF Empty(@name) THEN SET @name = "Member" ENDIF
          </div>
          <p style="font-size:0.82rem; color:var(--text-secondary); margin:0 0 0.4rem 0;"><strong>When to use:</strong> To safely extract context attributes and combine them defensively with <code>Empty()</code>, gracefully handling data that might be missing.</p>
          <p style="font-size:0.8rem; color:var(--neon-green); margin:0;"><em>In a nutshell: Retrieves context attributes returning null when no data is found.</em></p>
        </div>

        <!-- FUNCTION 2: Empty -->
        <div style="background:var(--bg-card); border:1px solid var(--border-dim); border-radius:var(--radius); padding:1.25rem; border-top:3px solid var(--neon-pink);">
          <div style="font-family:var(--font-mono); font-size:0.95rem; color:var(--neon-pink); font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.4rem;">Empty(@var)</div>
          <p style="font-size:0.85rem; color:var(--text-secondary); margin:0 0 0.5rem 0;"><strong>What it does:</strong> Returns <code>True</code> when the evaluated value is an empty string <code>""</code> or <code>null</code>.</p>
          <div style="background:rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding:0.5rem; border-radius:4px; font-family:var(--font-mono); font-size:0.75rem; color:var(--text-primary); margin-bottom:0.5rem;">
            IF Empty(@name) THEN SET @name = "Member" ENDIF
          </div>
          <p style="font-size:0.82rem; color:var(--text-secondary); margin:0 0 0.4rem 0;"><strong>When to use:</strong> Immediately after <code>AttributeValue()</code> to check if an attribute lacks data and assign a safe fallback default.</p>
          <p style="font-size:0.8rem; color:var(--neon-green); margin:0;"><em>In a nutshell: Tests whether a value is blank or null.</em></p>
        </div>

        <!-- FUNCTION 3: v -->
        <div style="background:var(--bg-card); border:1px solid var(--border-dim); border-radius:var(--radius); padding:1.25rem; border-top:3px solid var(--neon-yellow);">
          <div style="font-family:var(--font-mono); font-size:0.95rem; color:var(--neon-yellow); font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.4rem;">v(@var)</div>
          <p style="font-size:0.85rem; color:var(--text-secondary); margin:0 0 0.5rem 0;"><strong>What it does:</strong> Returns the value stored inside a variable to render it into the content output.</p>
          <div style="background:rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding:0.5rem; border-radius:4px; font-family:var(--font-mono); font-size:0.75rem; color:var(--text-primary); margin-bottom:0.5rem;">
            &lt;h1&gt;Hello, %%=v(@name)=%%&lt;/h1&gt;
          </div>
          <p style="font-size:0.82rem; color:var(--text-secondary); margin:0 0 0.4rem 0;"><strong>When to use:</strong> Anywhere in your HTML markup or text body where you want to output a variable's value.</p>
          <p style="font-size:0.8rem; color:var(--neon-green); margin:0;"><em>In a nutshell: Returns the value stored in a variable for printing.</em></p>
        </div>

        <!-- FUNCTION 4: Concat -->
        <div style="background:var(--bg-card); border:1px solid var(--border-dim); border-radius:var(--radius); padding:1.25rem; border-top:3px solid var(--neon-green);">
          <div style="font-family:var(--font-mono); font-size:0.95rem; color:var(--neon-green); font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.4rem;">Concat("Text1", @var, "Text2")</div>
          <p style="font-size:0.85rem; color:var(--text-secondary); margin:0 0 0.5rem 0;"><strong>What it does:</strong> Joins multiple string fragments, variables, or numbers into a single combined string.</p>
          <div style="background:rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding:0.5rem; border-radius:4px; font-family:var(--font-mono); font-size:0.75rem; color:var(--text-primary); margin-bottom:0.5rem;">
            SET @fullName = Concat(@first, " ", @last)
          </div>
          <p style="font-size:0.82rem; color:var(--text-secondary); margin:0 0 0.4rem 0;"><strong>When to use:</strong> To construct composite URLs, full names, or dynamic tracking parameters.</p>
          <p style="font-size:0.8rem; color:var(--neon-green); margin:0;"><em>In a nutshell: Combines multiple text strings and variables into one.</em></p>
        </div>

        <!-- FUNCTION 5: ProperCase -->
        <div style="background:var(--bg-card); border:1px solid var(--border-dim); border-radius:var(--radius); padding:1.25rem; border-top:3px solid var(--neon-purple);">
          <div style="font-family:var(--font-mono); font-size:0.95rem; color:var(--neon-purple); font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.4rem;">ProperCase(@var)</div>
          <p style="font-size:0.85rem; color:var(--text-secondary); margin:0 0 0.5rem 0;"><strong>What it does:</strong> Transforms text into proper case format, capitalizing the first letter of each word.</p>
          <div style="background:rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding:0.5rem; border-radius:4px; font-family:var(--font-mono); font-size:0.75rem; color:var(--text-primary); margin-bottom:0.5rem;">
            SET @cleanName = ProperCase(@rawName)
          </div>
          <p style="font-size:0.82rem; color:var(--text-secondary); margin:0 0 0.4rem 0;"><strong>When to use:</strong> To normalize the capitalization of subscriber names or text entered in inconsistent casing.</p>
          <p style="font-size:0.8rem; color:var(--neon-green); margin:0;"><em>In a nutshell: Capitalizes the first letter of each word in a string.</em></p>
        </div>

        <!-- FUNCTION 6: FormatDate -->
        <div style="background:var(--bg-card); border:1px solid var(--border-dim); border-radius:var(--radius); padding:1.25rem; border-top:3px solid var(--neon-cyan);">
          <div style="font-family:var(--font-mono); font-size:0.95rem; color:var(--neon-cyan); font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.4rem;">FormatDate(Date, "Pattern")</div>
          <p style="font-size:0.85rem; color:var(--text-secondary); margin:0 0 0.5rem 0;"><strong>What it does:</strong> Gives display formatting to a date according to standard pattern strings (e.g., <code>"yyyy-MM-dd"</code> or <code>"MM/dd/yyyy"</code>).</p>
          <div style="background:rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding:0.5rem; border-radius:4px; font-family:var(--font-mono); font-size:0.75rem; color:var(--text-primary); margin-bottom:0.5rem;">
            SET @today = FormatDate(Now(), "yyyy-MM-dd")
          </div>
          <p style="font-size:0.82rem; color:var(--text-secondary); margin:0 0 0.4rem 0;"><strong>When to use:</strong> To present expiration dates or purchase timestamps in a clean visual format. <em>(Note: FormatDate formats dates visually; it does not perform timezone conversions, for which specialized functions like <code>SystemDateToLocalDate()</code> exist).</em></p>
          <p style="font-size:0.8rem; color:var(--neon-green); margin:0;"><em>In a nutshell: Applies readable visual formatting to dates and times.</em></p>
        </div>

        <!-- FUNCTION 7: RedirectTo -->
        <div style="background:var(--bg-card); border:1px solid var(--border-dim); border-radius:var(--radius); padding:1.25rem; border-top:3px solid var(--neon-orange); grid-column: 1 / -1;">
          <div style="font-family:var(--font-mono); font-size:0.95rem; color:var(--neon-orange); font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.4rem;">RedirectTo(@dynamicUrl)</div>
          <p style="font-size:0.85rem; color:var(--text-secondary); margin:0 0 0.5rem 0;"><strong>What it does:</strong> Allows properly utilizing a dynamic URL from a variable, attribute, or field as a link destination in an email. In HTML emails, it is used inside the <code>href</code> attribute of an <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> tag and helps preserve click tracking when implemented this way.</p>
          <div style="background:rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding:0.5rem; border-radius:4px; font-family:var(--font-mono); font-size:0.75rem; color:var(--text-primary); margin-bottom:0.5rem;">
            &lt;a href="%%=RedirectTo(@checkoutUrl)=%%"&gt;Complete Purchase&lt;/a&gt;
          </div>
          <p style="font-size:0.82rem; color:var(--text-secondary); margin:0 0 0.4rem 0;"><strong>When to use:</strong> When creating dynamic links in emails where the target URL is computed via AMPscript. <em>(The &lt;a&gt; element creates the visual link or button; RedirectTo provides and resolves the dynamic destination).</em></p>
          <p style="font-size:0.8rem; color:var(--neon-green); margin:0;"><em>In a nutshell: Helps preserve click tracking on links built with dynamic variables.</em></p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <div class="callout tip">
        <div class="callout-label">💡 IN A NUTSHELL</div>
        These functions constitute the essential toolkit for safely retrieving, sanitizing, formatting, and rendering dynamic subscriber data.
      </div>

      <!-- SECCIÓN 08 · CASOS DE USO REALES -->
      <h2 id="use-cases-en">08 · REAL-WORLD USE CASES & DATA FLOW</h2>
      <p>To see how all these concepts connect together, let's examine the end-to-end personalization flow:</p>

      <div style="background:var(--bg-card); border:1px solid var(--border-dim); border-radius:var(--radius); padding:1rem; margin:1rem 0; text-align:center; font-family:var(--font-arcade); font-size:0.55rem; color:var(--neon-cyan); letter-spacing:1px;">
        INPUT DATA &nbsp;→&nbsp; AMPSCRIPT &nbsp;→&nbsp; CONDITIONAL DECISION &nbsp;→&nbsp; OUTPUT CONTENT
      </div>

      <!-- CASE 1 EN -->
      <div class="usecase-box">
        <div class="usecase-box-header">
          <span>✔️</span>
          <span>CASE 1: Dynamic greeting with defensive fallback</span>
        </div>
        <p style="font-size:0.88rem; color:var(--text-secondary); margin:0 0 0.8rem 0;">
          <strong>Scenario:</strong> If <code>FirstName</code> contains data, normalize it with <code>ProperCase()</code> and prefix a cordial greeting (<em>"Hello, Anna!"</em>). If it lacks data or is null, fall back cleanly to <em>"Valued Member"</em> without redundant wording.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block" style="margin:0.5rem 0;">
          <div class="code-block-header">
            <div class="code-block-dots"><div class="code-block-dot"></div><div class="code-block-dot"></div><div class="code-block-dot"></div></div>
            <span class="code-block-lang">AMPscript · Defensive Greeting</span>
            <button class="code-copy-btn">📋 Copy</button>
          </div>
          <pre><span class="cm">%%[
  VAR @rawName, @greeting

  SET @rawName = AttributeValue("FirstName")

  IF Empty(@rawName) THEN
    SET @greeting = "Valued Member"
  ELSE
    SET @greeting = Concat("Hello, ", ProperCase(@rawName), "!")
  ENDIF
]%%</span>

<span class="cm">&lt;!-- HTML Output --&gt;</span>
<span class="kw">&lt;h2&gt;</span><span class="st">%%=v(@greeting)=%%</span><span class="kw">&lt;/h2&gt;</span></pre>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- CASE 2 EN -->
      <div class="usecase-box">
        <div class="usecase-box-header">
          <span>✔️</span>
          <span>CASE 2: Tiered offers in an Abandoned Cart campaign</span>
        </div>
        <p style="font-size:0.88rem; color:var(--text-secondary); margin:0 0 0.8rem 0;">
          <strong>Scenario:</strong> Evaluate cart total and VIP membership to assign a tiered voucher and generate a trackable CTA button.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block" style="margin:0.5rem 0;">
          <div class="code-block-header">
            <div class="code-block-dots"><div class="code-block-dot"></div><div class="code-block-dot"></div><div class="code-block-dot"></div></div>
            <span class="code-block-lang">AMPscript · Cart Logic</span>
            <button class="code-copy-btn">📋 Copy</button>
          </div>
          <pre><span class="cm">%%[
  VAR @cartTotal, @tier, @discount, @checkoutUrl

  SET @cartTotal = AttributeValue("CartTotal")
  SET @tier = AttributeValue("CustomerTier")
  SET @checkoutUrl = "https://store.example.com/checkout?id=cart123"

  IF @tier == "VIP" AND @cartTotal &gt; 100 THEN
    SET @discount = 25
  ELSEIF @cartTotal &gt; 50 THEN
    SET @discount = 15
  ELSE
    SET @discount = 5
  ENDIF
]%%</span>

<span class="cm">&lt;!-- HTML Output with RedirectTo --&gt;</span>
<span class="kw">&lt;p&gt;</span>You have an exclusive <span class="st">%%=v(@discount)=%%</span>% discount voucher.<span class="kw">&lt;/p&gt;</span>
<span class="kw">&lt;a</span> <span class="vr">href</span>=<span class="st">"%%=RedirectTo(@checkoutUrl)=%%"</span> <span class="vr">class</span>=<span class="st">"btn-cta"</span><span class="kw">&gt;</span>Complete My Order<span class="kw">&lt;/a&gt;</span></pre>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="callout tip">
        <div class="callout-label">💡 IN A NUTSHELL</div>
        Combining defensive data extraction, Empty() validation, and conditionals helps create robust personalized customer experiences.
      </div>

      <!-- SECTION 09 EN -->
      <h2 id="best-practices">09 · GOLDEN RULES FOR AMPSCRIPT DEVELOPMENT</h2>
      <p>When developing in AMPscript, it is vital to distinguish between <strong>mandatory syntax requirements</strong> (such as using valid comparison operators like <code>==</code> in conditionals) and <strong>recommended best practices</strong> that improve maintainability and help prevent production errors:</p>

      <ol style="padding-left:1.2rem; line-height:1.8; color:var(--text-secondary); font-size:0.9rem;">
        <li><strong style="color:var(--text-primary);">Declare variables with <code>VAR</code>:</strong> While AMPscript allows on-the-fly variable instantiation, explicitly declaring them at the top makes scripts clean, readable, and easy to maintain.</li>
        <li><strong style="color:var(--text-primary);">Use <code>AttributeValue()</code> for context attributes:</strong> A direct attribute reference that cannot be resolved (like <code>[FieldName]</code>) can trigger rendering errors. <code>AttributeValue()</code> provides a defensive way to fetch values by returning <code>null</code> when no data is found, allowing clean validation with <code>Empty()</code>.</li>
        <li><strong style="color:var(--text-primary);">Validate important data with <code>Empty()</code>:</strong> Validating with <code>Empty()</code> reduces the risk of errors caused by blank or null data by enabling safe fallback defaults.</li>
        <li><strong style="color:var(--text-primary);">Centralize logic when possible:</strong> Preparing variables in a primary block at the top of your email helps keep HTML clean, while keeping component render order in mind.</li>
        <li><strong style="color:var(--text-primary);">Use <code>==</code> to compare equality:</strong> Remember that <code>=</code> assigns a value, while <code>==</code> checks if two values are equal.</li>
        <li><strong style="color:var(--text-primary);">Test multiple subscriber profiles in "Preview and Test":</strong> Verify behavior against real contact records with empty attributes, null fields, and edge-case values.</li>
        <li><strong style="color:var(--text-primary);">Use <code>RedirectTo()</code> for dynamic links in emails:</strong> When you need to preserve click tracking on URLs originating from variables or attributes, wrap the variable inside the <code>href</code> attribute.</li>
        <li><strong style="color:var(--text-primary);">Do not assume input data is clean:</strong> Choose helper functions based on the specific issue. For example, <code>Trim()</code> removes leading and trailing whitespace, while <code>ProperCase()</code> easily normalizes capitalization.</li>
      </ol>

      <div class="callout tip">
        <div class="callout-label">💡 IN A NUTSHELL</div>
        Defensive programming and clean separation of logic from HTML presentation improve maintainability and help prevent issues during mass sends.
      </div>

      <!-- SECTION 10 EN · SELF-ASSESSMENT -->
      <h2 id="self-assessment">10 · CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING</h2>
      <p>Test your grasp of core AMPscript concepts before tackling the final challenge:</p>

      <div style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:0.8rem; margin:1.5rem 0;">
        
        <details style="background:var(--bg-elevated); border:1px solid var(--border-dim); border-radius:var(--radius); padding:1rem;">
          <summary style="cursor:pointer; font-weight:700; color:var(--neon-cyan); font-size:0.9rem;">
            1. What is the difference between "=" and "==" in AMPscript?
          </summary>
          <div style="margin-top:0.8rem; font-size:0.85rem; color:var(--text-secondary); line-height:1.6; border-top:1px solid var(--border-dim); padding-top:0.8rem;">
            <strong>Answer:</strong> The <code>=</code> operator is used for <em>assignment</em> to store a value in a variable (e.g., <code>SET @tier = "VIP"</code>). The <code>==</code> operator is used for <em>comparison</em> to test equality in a condition (e.g., <code>IF @tier == "VIP" THEN</code>).
          </div>
        </details>

        <details style="background:var(--bg-elevated); border:1px solid var(--border-dim); border-radius:var(--radius); padding:1rem;">
          <summary style="cursor:pointer; font-weight:700; color:var(--neon-pink); font-size:0.9rem;">
            2. When should you use %%[ ]%% vs %%= =%%?
          </summary>
          <div style="margin-top:0.8rem; font-size:0.85rem; color:var(--text-secondary); line-height:1.6; border-top:1px solid var(--border-dim); padding-top:0.8rem;">
            <strong>Answer:</strong> <code>%%[ ... ]%%</code> is primarily used for code blocks that execute logic server-side (declaring variables, assignments, conditionals). <code>%%= ... =%%</code> is used inline in HTML to print/render the value of a variable or function in the message.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details style="background:var(--bg-elevated); border:1px solid var(--border-dim); border-radius:var(--radius); padding:1rem;">
          <summary style="cursor:pointer; font-weight:700; color:var(--neon-yellow); font-size:0.9rem;">
            3. Why is combining AttributeValue() with Empty() a best practice?
          </summary>
          <div style="margin-top:0.8rem; font-size:0.85rem; color:var(--text-secondary); line-height:1.6; border-top:1px solid var(--border-dim); padding-top:0.8rem;">
            <strong>Answer:</strong> <code>AttributeValue()</code> retrieves the context attribute returning <code>null</code> when no data is found, and <code>Empty()</code> tests whether that result is blank or null so you can apply a safe fallback default.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details style="background:var(--bg-elevated); border:1px solid var(--border-dim); border-radius:var(--radius); padding:1rem;">
          <summary style="cursor:pointer; font-weight:700; color:var(--neon-green); font-size:0.9rem;">
            4. What is the difference between VAR, SET, and v()?
          </summary>
          <div style="margin-top:0.8rem; font-size:0.85rem; color:var(--text-secondary); line-height:1.6; border-top:1px solid var(--border-dim); padding-top:0.8rem;">
            <strong>Answer:</strong> <code>VAR</code> declares the variable (creates the labeled box), <code>SET</code> assigns a value (places data into the box), and <code>v()</code> returns the stored value of a variable to render it in the final output.
          </div>
        </details>

      </div>

      <div class="callout tip">
        <div class="callout-label">💡 IN A NUTSHELL</div>
        VAR creates the variable, SET assigns a value, conditionals evaluate rules using ==, and v() displays the result.
      </div>

      <!-- SECTION 11 · ARCADE CHALLENGE EN -->
      <h2 id="challenge-en">11 · ARCADE CHALLENGE: YOUR FIRST DYNAMIC GREETING</h2>
      <div class="challenge-box">
        <div class="challenge-box-header">
          <span class="challenge-box-icon">🎮 </span>
          <div>
            <div class="challenge-box-title">MISSION: Personalized Greeting with Dynamic Discount</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;color:var(--text-secondary);margin-bottom:1rem;">
          Create an AMPscript block that processes subscriber name (<code>FirstName</code>) and loyalty tier (<code>Tier</code>):
        </p>
        <ul style="font-size:0.88rem;color:var(--text-secondary);margin-bottom:1rem;">
          <li>Safely extract <code>FirstName</code> and <code>Tier</code> using <code>AttributeValue()</code>.</li>
          <li>If the name exists, format it with <code>ProperCase()</code> and build the concatenated greeting (e.g., <code>Concat("Hello, ", ProperCase(@rawName), "!")</code>); if blank or null, use the fallback <em>"Valued Member"</em> with <code>Empty()</code>.</li>
          <li>Evaluate <code>Tier</code> using strict equality with <code>==</code>: If <code>Tier == "VIP"</code>, assign 20% off; if <code>Tier == "Gold"</code>, assign 10%; otherwise, assign 5%.</li>
          <li>Output the personalized greeting and discount percentage in the HTML using <code>v()</code>.</li>
        </ul>

        <details style="font-family:var(--font-mono);font-size:0.75rem;">
          <summary style="cursor:pointer;color:var(--neon-yellow);margin-bottom:0.75rem;"> View full solution</summary>
          <div class="code-block" style="margin-top:0.75rem;">
            <div class="code-block-header">
              <div class="code-block-dots"><div class="code-block-dot"></div><div class="code-block-dot"></div><div class="code-block-dot"></div></div>
              <span class="code-block-lang">Full Solution · AMPscript</span>
              <button class="code-copy-btn">📋 Copy</button>
            </div>
            <pre><span class="cm">%%[
  /* 1. Declare all variables with VAR */</span>
  <span class="kw">VAR</span> <span class="vr">@rawName</span>, <span class="vr">@greeting</span>, <span class="vr">@tier</span>, <span class="vr">@discountPercent</span>

  <span class="cm">/* 2. Safely extract subscriber context data */</span>
  <span class="kw">SET</span> <span class="vr">@rawName</span> = <span class="fn">AttributeValue</span>(<span class="st">"FirstName"</span>)
  <span class="kw">SET</span> <span class="vr">@tier</span> = <span class="fn">AttributeValue</span>(<span class="st">"Tier"</span>)

  <span class="cm">/* 3. Validate name with Empty() and build greeting */</span>
  <span class="kw">IF Empty</span>(<span class="vr">@rawName</span>) <span class="kw">THEN</span>
    <span class="kw">SET</span> <span class="vr">@greeting</span> = <span class="st">"Valued Member"</span>
  <span class="kw">ELSE</span>
    <span class="kw">SET</span> <span class="vr">@greeting</span> = <span class="fn">Concat</span>(<span class="st">"Hello, "</span>, <span class="fn">ProperCase</span>(<span class="vr">@rawName</span>), <span class="st">"!"</span>)
  <span class="kw">ENDIF</span>

  <span class="cm">/* 4. Evaluate membership tier using == */</span>
  <span class="kw">IF</span> <span class="vr">@tier</span> == <span class="st">"VIP"</span> <span class="kw">THEN</span>
    <span class="kw">SET</span> <span class="vr">@discountPercent</span> = <span class="nb">20</span>
  <span class="kw">ELSEIF</span> <span class="vr">@tier</span> == <span class="st">"Gold"</span> <span class="kw">THEN</span>
    <span class="kw">SET</span> <span class="vr">@discountPercent</span> = <span class="nb">10</span>
  <span class="kw">ELSE</span>
    <span class="kw">SET</span> <span class="vr">@discountPercent</span> = <span class="nb">5</span>
  <span class="kw">ENDIF</span>
<span class="cm">]%%</span>

<span class="cm">&lt;!-- Rendered HTML --&gt;</span>
<span class="kw">&lt;div style="background:#1a1d2e; border:1px solid #00ffff; border-radius:8px; padding:20px; font-family:sans-serif; color:#ffffff;"&gt;</span>
  <span class="kw">&lt;h2 style="color:#00ffff; margin-top:0;"&gt;</span><span class="st">%%=v(@greeting)=%%</span><span class="kw">&lt;/h2&gt;</span>
  <span class="kw">&lt;p&gt;</span>As a valued member of our community, you have a special perk:<span class="kw">&lt;/p&gt;</span>
  <span class="kw">&lt;div style="font-size:24px; font-weight:bold; color:#ff00ff;"&gt;</span>
    🎟️ <span class="st">%%=v(@discountPercent)=%%</span>% OFF
  <span class="kw">&lt;/div&gt;</span>
<span class="kw">&lt;/div&gt;</span></pre>
          </div>
          <p style="margin-top:0.8rem; font-size:0.85rem; color:var(--text-secondary);">
            <strong>Breakdown:</strong> We use <code>AttributeValue()</code> to retrieve attributes defensively. With <code>Empty()</code> we provide a safe greeting fallback stored inside <code>@greeting</code>, format with <code>ProperCase()</code>, and evaluate <code>@tier</code> using strict equality <code>==</code> comparisons.
          </p>
        </details>
      </div>

    </div>

